# My Recommendation for You ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Since you've started building Figma designs, I recommend creating **Frontend Boilerplate v1.0** and using it for every project.

As you learn more, you'll keep improving it.

Eventually, you'll have a professional boilerplate with:

* ✅ HTML5 Structure
* ✅ CSS Variables
* ✅ Modern Reset
* ✅ Responsive Container
* ✅ Utility Classes
* ✅ Buttons
* ✅ Cards
* ✅ Forms
* ✅ Navigation
* ✅ Footer
* ✅ Animations
* ✅ 12-column Grid
* ✅ Flex Utilities
* ✅ Spacing Utilities
* ✅ Dark Mode Support

This is exactly how many professional frontend developers work—they start every project from a refined boilerplate instead of creating everything from scratch.
Excellent question. **Modifier classes** are one of the most important CSS concepts used in professional development.
The idea is:

> **One common class contains shared styles, and modifier classes change only what is different.**


# Example 1: Button Size Modifier
### CSS
/* Common Button */
.btn{
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 16px 32px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: #FD6E0A;
    color: #fff;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
}

/* Modifier */
.btn-lg{
    padding: 22px 40px;
    font-size: 22px;
}

.btn-sm{
    padding: 10px 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
}


### HTML
<a class="btn">Normal</a>
<a class="btn btn-lg">Large</a>
<a class="btn btn-sm">Small</a>


# Example 2: Button Color Modifier
### CSS
.btn{
    padding:16px 32px;
    border-radius:5px;
    color:#fff;
}

.btn-primary{
    background:#FD6E0A;
}

.btn-dark{
    background:#181818;
}

.btn-success{
    background:green;
}

### HTML
<a class="btn btn-primary">Hire Me</a>
<a class="btn btn-dark">Read More</a>
<a class="btn btn-success">Submit</a>


# Example 3: Card Modifier
### CSS
.card{
    padding:30px;
    border-radius:10px;
    background:#fff;
    box-shadow:0 5px 20px rgba(0,0,0,.1);
}
.card-featured{
    background:#FD6E0A;
    color:#fff;
}
.card-dark{
    background:#181818;
    color:#fff;
}


### HTML

<div class="card">
    Normal Card
</div>

<div class="card card-featured">
    Featured Card
</div>

<div class="card card-dark">
    Dark Card
</div>

# Example 4: Section Title Modifier

### CSS
.section-title{
    font-size:40px;
    font-weight:700;
}

.section-title-white{
    color:#fff;
}

.section-title-center{
    text-align:center;
}

.section-title-lg{
    font-size:60px;
}

### HTML
<h2 class="section-title">
    About Me
</h2>

<h2 class="section-title section-title-center">
    Services
</h2>

<h2 class="section-title section-title-white">
    Contact
</h2>

<h2 class="section-title section-title-lg">
    Hero Title
</h2>

# Example 5: Container Modifier

### CSS
.container{
    width:100%;
    max-width:1140px;
    margin:auto;
}

.container-lg{
    max-width:1320px;
}

.container-sm{
    max-width:900px;
}

### HTML

<div class="container">
    Default Width
</div>

<div class="container container-lg">
    Large Container
</div>

<div class="container container-sm">
    Small Container
</div>

# Example 6: Flex Modifier
### CSS

.flex{
    display:flex;
}

.flex-center{
    justify-content:center;
    align-items:center;
}

.flex-between{
    justify-content:space-between;
}

.flex-column{
    flex-direction:column;
}

### HTML

<div class="flex flex-between">
</div>

<div class="flex flex-center">
</div>

<div class="flex flex-column">
</div>

# Example 7: Margin Modifier

### CSS

.mt-20{
    margin-top:20px;
}

.mt-40{
    margin-top:40px;
}

.mb-30{
    margin-bottom:30px;
}


### HTML
<h2 class="section-title mt-40">
    About
</h2>

<p class="mb-30">
    Paragraph
</p>

# Professional Naming Pattern

.btn              ← Base class

.btn-primary      ← Color modifier
.btn-secondary
.btn-outline

.btn-sm           ← Size modifier
.btn-md
.btn-lg

.card             ← Base class

.card-dark        ← Modifier
.card-featured

.container        ← Base class

.container-lg     ← Modifier
```

---

# Real-World Example

.btn{
    display:inline-block;
    padding:16px 32px;
    border-radius:5px;
    text-decoration:none;
    font-weight:600;
}

/* Color */
.btn-primary{
    background:#FD6E0A;
    color:#fff;
}

.btn-outline{
    border:2px solid #FD6E0A;
    color:#FD6E0A;
}

/* Size */
.btn-lg{
    padding:20px 40px;
    font-size:22px;
}

.btn-sm{
    padding:10px 20px;
    font-size:14px;
}

### HTML

<a class="btn btn-primary">Hire Me</a>
<a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Download CV</a>
<a class="btn btn-outline">Contact</a>
<a class="btn btn-outline btn-sm">Read More</a>


## Rule to Remember

Think of it like this:

Base Class
    ↓
Provides the common styles

Modifier Class
    ↓
Changes only the differences


This pattern keeps your CSS clean, reusable, and easy to maintain, which is why it's widely used in professional projects.
